#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068460 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068924 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bc85ca17-8a08-5473-9473-dce954132bd5" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Y-Intercept of a Line Given a Point on the Line and Its Slope"}] :question/id #uuid "4ad21d3a-bf40-5b65-8ba6-320d853e6cff" :question/math-academy-id "e-12869" :question/problem "Find the $y$-intercept of the line that passes through the point $(-1, 4)$ and has a slope of $2$." :question/worked-solution "Since the line passes through the point $(-1,4)$ and its slope is $2$, we substitute $x=-1$, $y=4$, and $m=2$ into the equation in slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} y &= mx + b \\\\ (4) &= (2)(-1) + b \\\\ 4 &= -2 + b \\\\ 6 &= b \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the $y$-intercept is $b=6$. The coordinates of the $y$-intercept are $(0,6)$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068461 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068925 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5e985ae8-05ba-590a-b504-4c84884679f4" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Equation of a Line That Passes Through Two Points"}] :question/id #uuid "fdc3a6e9-080b-5e2a-b5a4-3af01c7018ff" :question/math-academy-id "e-12870" :question/problem "Find the equation of the line that passes through the points $(-2,4)$ and $(10,10)$." :question/worked-solution "We will write the equation of the line in the slope-intercept form\n\n$$\ny=mx+b\n$$\n\nSo, we need to work out the slope $m$ and the $y$-intercept $b$.\n\nFirst, we calculate the slope $m$ using the given points:\n\n$$\nm = \\frac{y_{2} - y_{1}}{x_{2} - x_{1}} = (10 - 4)/(10 - (-2)) = \\frac{6}{12} = \\frac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the slope\n\n$$\nm=\\dfrac 1 2\n$$\n\ninto the slope-intercept formula\n\n$$\ny=mx+b\n$$\n\nwe reach\n\n$$\ny = \\dfrac 1 2 x + b\n$$\n\nWe now need to find the $y$-intercept $b$. We can find this by substituting the coordinates of a point on the line. Any point will do, so let's substitute $(10,10)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &= \\frac{1}{2}x + b \\\\\n10 &= \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot 10 + b \\\\\n10 &= 5 + b \\\\\nb &= 10 - 5 \\\\\nb &= 5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the line is\n\n$$\ny=\\dfrac 1 2 x + 5\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068462 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068926 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "63461668-4f76-52a4-8115-1ab135eef9e3" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the X-Intercept of a Line Given a Point and a Slope"}] :question/id #uuid "ec5bebf6-58d8-5b4d-900c-3c024628647c" :question/math-academy-id "e-12871" :question/problem "Calculate the $x$-intercept of the line that passes through the point $(-3,2)$ and has a slope of $-\\dfrac{1}{2}$." :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the equation of the line. We know that\n\n$$\nm=-\\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nso we can write the equation of the line as\n\n$$\ny=-\\dfrac{1}{2}x + b\n$$\n\nIt remains to find the $y$-intercept of the line. We can do that by substituting the point $(-3,2)$ into the equation and solving for $b$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &= -\\frac{1}{2}x + b \\\\\n(2) &= -\\frac{1}{2}(-3) + b \\\\\n2 &= \\frac{3}{2} + b \\\\\n\\frac{1}{2} &= b\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equation of the line is\n\n$$\ny=-\\dfrac{1}{2}x+\\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nAt the $x$-intercept, the $y$-coordinate is equal to $0$. So, we can substitute $y=0$ into the equation and then solve for $x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &= -\\frac{1}{2}x + \\frac{1}{2} \\\\\n0 &= -\\frac{1}{2}x + \\frac{1}{2} \\\\\n\\frac{1}{2}x &= \\frac{1}{2} \\\\\nx &= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $x$-intercept is $x=1$. The coordinates of the $x$-intercept are $(1,0)$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068463 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068927 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f66989ce-aac5-5737-b60b-6ed703634cd5" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Intercept of a Horizontal or Vertical Line"}] :question/id #uuid "409dfb1f-7400-5539-9dea-6c2d1b6b7ef0" :question/math-academy-id "e-12872" :question/problem "A vertical line $l$ passes through the point $(-6,3)$. Calculate the coordinates of the $x$-intercept of $l$." :question/worked-solution "On any vertical line, all points have the same $x$-coordinate.\n\nThe $x$-coordinate of the point $(-6,3)$ is $-6$, so the $x$-coordinate of the $x$-intercept is also $-6$.\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the $x$-intercept are $(-6,0)$."}]}
